A||N||10||fropuff||Who has Dickman's sub?  ||||10||
Z||000000||fropuff||10-28-2000||08:45 PM||||Hey, I heard a little while ago that Rodney Dickman was working on a replacement sub.  I went on his site today, and it turns out he has and it is available.  I was wondering if anyone has bought the replacement sub from him.  I was wondering of the quality, sound, etc.  By the way, I do NOT have the OEM sub setup.  How much more work would this consist of?  I'm not really an expert when it comes to this stuff.  How would I go wiring it up and all that stuff?  Even though mine doesn't have the OEM setup, would the mounting holes/brackets still be there?  Thanks.||||reg||10
Z||000001||fropuff||10-28-2000||09:10 PM||||If anyone wants to see the sub/speaker, go to:  <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/retail.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/retail.html</A>   Then go to the "electrical" category.  It's one of the three in "audio".  I think it sounds like a good deal.  $32 for everything.  I am worried about me not having an OEM setup.  I heard that something in the courtesy lights has something to do with it.  I don't know.  Can someone help me?  Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by fropuff (edited 10-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-28-2000||10:19 PM||||His sub is a really good quality one. If you interested in a high-end one though, JL Audio recently came out with a 5.25" sub with high power handling capability. I comes at price though. For the money, his is great. If your interested, I am doing a harness that plugs in between the factory harness and radio and then goes to the sub. It that same as how the factory ones are hooked up, just not ran all over the car like the stock ones are. If you can find yourself a subbox and get the sub for it, I can provide you with the stock amp, and wiring harness to go between and hook up. LMK through email if you have any questions. sean@kdsi.net<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||10-28-2000||11:39 PM||||Sounds good to me.  I e-mailed you.  Does anyone know what the OEM sub box looks like?  Any pictures available?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||speed racer||10-29-2000||12:37 AM||||Does any one know if it sounds better than orginal one||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||10-29-2000||11:01 AM||||Yeah, I heard someone say it was a little improvement over the OEM sub.  It would be better for me, as I don't even have that.  Now, does anyone know about building a box for it?  All I need is a picture of it.  Would I need to build a ported box or a sealed box?  I'd imagine a sealed.  Do I have the sub facing the floor?  Thanks.  That's all I need to know to build one.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||10-29-2000||11:21 AM||||The speaker itself is actually mounted on one of the sides, I believe it's the side facing the center tunnel. Remember also that the enclosure has a tuned port that I feel would be difficult to duplicate, even with a model to work off of. My personal opinion is that if you are going through the effort of building your own enclosure it would be worth the extra $ to get a proper sub (8" to 10") and fabricate an enclosure in the footwell.  Also Seanh apparently has made an enclosure for an 8 that fits in the stock sub location.  Perhaps Seanh can give some more details on that hook-up, sounds like a good idea.<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fropuff||10-29-2000||12:42 PM||||I guess I might have to buy a stock enclosure, but I don't really have the cash to do that.  I guess I'll have to wait.  I can't do anything anymore.  Stupid Cutlass.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||10-29-2000||01:05 PM||||The OEM sub box has a long port tube that comes out the top and extends over toward the center of the dash. The speaker is mounted in the bottom and fires toward the floor. The box is a very irregular shape and would be hard to duplicate without making a mold from an original.<BR> I have a case of Pioneer subs that are a direct replacement for the OEM Firebird subs. I don't think they fit the Fiero enclosure without modification, they are about 6". I will sell them for $10 each if you want one.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000009||286SE'S||10-30-2000||03:23 PM||||The Performance sound option was offered in 1986 and there is a slight change to the convenience center (aka "blue dingy thingy")<BR>mounting to make space for it. If you car is 84-85 your dingy thingy is facing toward the right side, after 86 it faces down. You would probably need the cover to the heater core with the correct bracket when you add the subwoofer. <BR> I am curious about this statement:<BR>Seanh wrote:<BR> "It that same as how the factory ones are hooked up, just not ran all over the car like the stock ones are."<BR>Does this mean you do not use the overhead control? How do you adjust the level? I have a sub and amp (spares) but no overhead control.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||10-30-2000||05:47 PM||||I put a variable 10K resistor next to the cig. lighter in the trim plate. The volum control is a variable 10K resistor in series with a fixed 10K resistor.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit3_1.gif">   <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
